3. PROHIBITED ACTIVITIES

Without such a separate written agreement, no authorization is granted to:

(a) access, download, reproduce, display, perform, or distribute the Materials
outside the limited scope required by the applicable hosting platform;

(b) execute, run, compile, test, evaluate, validate, benchmark, profile,
compare, analyze, or otherwise use the Materials or their outputs;

(c) modify, translate, adapt, or prepare derivative works based upon the
copyrightable expression in the Materials;

(d) reverse engineer, decompile, disassemble, decode, circumvent, or attempt to
derive source code, nonpublic design information, architecture, models,
parameters, methods, or implementation details, except only to the extent a
specific restriction is prohibited by nonwaivable applicable law;

(e) sublicense, sell, rent, lease, transfer, publish, or otherwise make the
Materials available to another person or entity;

(f) deploy or use the Materials to provide, operate, develop, train, support,
or improve a commercial product or service;

(g) scrape, crawl, data-mine, index, ingest, or use the Materials or their
outputs to train, fine-tune, evaluate, benchmark, or improve an artificial
intelligence system, machine-learning system, dataset, model, or competing
technology, except for rights that Matrix Solutions LLC has granted to the
applicable hosting platform under binding platform terms; or

(h) remove, conceal, or alter an applicable copyright, patent, trademark,
attribution, or proprietary-rights notice.

Nothing in this notice claims exclusive rights in ideas, facts, discoveries,
mathematical relationships, procedures, systems, methods of operation, or
independently developed materials that applicable law does not protect through
copyright.

4. EXPRESS VALIDATION AUTHORIZATION

Validation is permitted only when Matrix Solutions LLC issues a separate,
express written validation authorization signed by an authorized
representative of Matrix Solutions LLC (a “Validation Authorization”). An
email, conversation, repository issue, pull request, social-media message,
course of dealing, access credential, delivery of Materials, or informal
statement is not a Validation Authorization unless it contains or attaches the
signed authorization.

A Validation Authorization must identify, at minimum:

(a) the authorized individual or legal entity;

(b) the precise Materials, repository, version, release, or commit covered;

(c) the validation question, protocol, permitted inputs, datasets, arguments,
commands, and computing environment;

(d) the authorized duration and any access, security, confidentiality,
retention, and destruction requirements; and

(e) whether and how results, logs, reports, quotations, screenshots, derived
data, or conclusions may be disclosed or published.

Unless a Validation Authorization expressly states otherwise, it is personal,
limited, nonexclusive, nontransferable, nonsublicensable, revocable, and valid
only for the identified validation. It grants no commercial, production,
training, competitive, redistribution, publication, derivative-work, patent,
trademark, or ownership rights.

An authorized validator may not exceed the express scope of the Validation
Authorization, provide access to another person, substitute different data or
arguments, retain copies beyond the authorized period, or publish results
without express written permission. Silence or failure to object does not
expand the authorization.

Unless the Validation Authorization expressly provides otherwise, permission
terminates automatically upon expiration, completion of the authorized
validation, or breach, whichever occurs first. Upon termination, the validator
must immediately stop using the Materials and permanently delete or return all
copies, outputs, credentials, and nonpublic information as directed by Matrix
Solutions LLC, and must certify compliance in writing if requested.

